     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 12,750 mg/day of metformin is about 5 times the maximum daily dose. I know of no indication that would justify it, and it could cause severe lactic acidosis in a 70 kg patient even with normal kidney function. Because of that risk, I'm not comfortable placing it as a routine order.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Standard dosing:** Give me a dose within the labeled range, such as 850 mg three times daily or 1,000 mg twice daily, and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Unusual clinical scenario:** If there's a reason for this dose that I don't know about, please talk with the pharmacy or clinical pharmacology service first. Then enter the order through your hospital's high-dose override process, with the rationale documented.\n- **Possible overdose or ingestion:** If this relates to an overdose, that needs toxicology and poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US). It isn't a standing order.\n\nI can also add a note to a corrected order, for example \"titrate to glycemic response.\""
      }
     ],
